From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add the DisplayPort AUX/HPD PHY node

One node, the device-tree side of patch `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`: it describes the DP AUX/HPD analog
front end at 0x06C01E00 -- three registers inside the SERDES subsystem aperture,
AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL0/1/2, UM 18.2.6.12-18.2.6.14, listed as +0x1e00 in
the user manual.

Kept separate from `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy` because it is separable: the driver is reviewable
against the UM field tables on its own, and the node is where the board-level
claims live.

reg
---

`<0x0 0x06c01e00 0x0 0x200>` -- base and length exactly as the block is laid out
in the UM register list (0x1e00, 0x1e04, 0x1e08, next block COMB1 at 0x2000) and
as the vendor DT describes it. Unit address 6c01e00, no collision with
combophy0's 6c01000.

⚠ The window sits *inside* combophy0's first window (0x06C01000 + 0x1000 from
`arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy`). That is not an oversight in this node and it is not fixed here: the
overlap is handled on the driver side, which maps non-exclusively, and the clean
fix -- shrinking combophy0's window to the 0xa00 it actually needs -- edits the
HW-proven USB3 node and belongs to a patch that re-proves SuperSpeed. The
reasoning is written out in `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`'s header and in the driver comment; this line
exists so a reader of the DT alone does not conclude the reg is wrong.

clocks / resets
---------------

`CLK_SERDES_PHY_CFG` and `RST_BUS_SERDES`, i.e. the SERDES-wide pair that
combophy0 (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` and `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy`) and serdes_ccu (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks`) already take. Two consequences,
both deliberate:

 - the reset is *shared*. Those two hold it deasserted for the running USB3
   controller; a node that could assert it would be able to take their registers
   away. reset_control_get_shared() on the driver side makes this node's
   deassert a refcount bump and its assert a decrement that never reaches zero
   while USB3 lives.
 - no `assigned-clock-rates`. combophy0 sets serdes-phy-cfg to 100 MHz in its
   phy_init; both PHYs run off that one net, so a rate here would be two drivers
   fighting over one clock.

No `dcxo-serdes0`: the vendor's aux_hpd init enables the serdes clock only, and
nothing in the AUX block's three registers references the DCXO.

No power-domain. combophy0 has none either (`arm64-dts-a733-usb3-power-domain` attached PD_USB2 to the dwc3
controller alone), and no domain id for the SERDES/DP side has a source.
Guessing one is exactly the class of value this tree does not
guess.

Board wiring the node stands for
--------------------------------

 - DP-AUXP/AUXN -> Type-C J4 SBU1/SBU2 through 100 nF DC blocking caps
   (C105/C107, sch p12 L38-44; SoC-side nets sch p6 L124-125).
 - **No external SBU mux and no redriver on this board.** HUSB311 is a CC/PD
   controller only. Whatever handles a
   reversed plug is inside the SoC.
 - DP-REXT = R68 3.01K (sch p6 L144-145).
 - DP-HPD (sch p6 L127) appears on no other schematic page, so it is not routed
   to the connector; in DP alt mode HPD arrives over CC as a DisplayPort
   Attention message.
 - The block's rail is AVDD-HPD-AUX, 3.3 V (sch p6 L130). Our
   rail map does not name its PMIC output (the
   regulator column is empty), so no `*-supply` is declared. A `regulator-fixed`
   stand-in would satisfy the binding and prove nothing.

status and the missing consumer
-------------------------------

`status = "okay"` with no consumer, and that is safe rather than sloppy: the
`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy` probe path maps the window, gets the clock and the reset, creates the phy
and returns without touching a register. The first write happens when something
calls phy_init()/phy_power_on(), and nothing does yet.

⚠ When that consumer arrives it must not call `phy_power_on()` at bind. The
vendor's DP stack does the equivalent and logs 34 AUX timeouts over 4.5 s on
every boot with nothing plugged into J4 (the 8 GiB board);
`phy_power_on()` is the point at which this front end's bandgap and AUX
enable come up, so it belongs after DP alt mode has been entered and the mux is
in a DP state, not at bind. Full reasoning and the measured numbers are in
`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`'s header and in the driver's file comment.

The label is `aux_hpd` so the DP transmitter can pick it up unchanged when it
lands. The vendor puts that consumer on its eDP node as

	phys = <&combo0_dp>, <&aux_hpd_phy>;
	phy-names = "dp-phy", "aux-phy";

and `aux_hpd_phy` there is the child node of an `aux-hpd@6c01e00` container; our
tree has no container, so the same wiring here reads

	phys = <&combo0_dp>, <&aux_hpd>;
	phy-names = "dp-phy", "aux-phy";

on the eDP0 node at 0x05740000 -- which this tree does not model yet, and which
needs its EDP_BGR gate in the CCU first (measured 2026-08-17: EDP_BGR_REG at
0x0200364C reads 0x00000000 on our kernel).

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 on top of the current series, dtc builds the dtb
with no new warning, and the compiled dtb carries phy@6c01e00 with its
compatible, reg, clock and reset. NOT verified: the board. What a board would
have to show is in `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`'s header -- the three registers reading 0x03331131 /
0x00010e0d / 0x00000000, and USB3 still enumerating at SuperSpeed afterwards.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1449,6 +1449,45 @@
 					remote-endpoint = <&usb_con_ss>;
 				};
 			};
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * DisplayPort AUX/HPD analog front end (patch `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`). Three registers
+		 * at SERDES_SUBSYS_APP + 0x1e00..0x1e08 (UM
+		 * 18.2.6.12-14): the AUX receiver/transmitter trims and enables, the
+		 * on-chip termination, and the HPD circuitry. It is its own PHY
+		 * instance in the UM block diagram, beside COMB0_PHY_SERDES rather
+		 * than inside it, and it is the block that makes AUX -- and therefore
+		 * DPCD, EDID and link training -- possible at all.
+		 *
+		 * Board side: DP-AUXP/AUXN go to Type-C J4 SBU1/SBU2 through 100 nF
+		 * DC blocking caps with no external mux or redriver
+		 * (sch p12 L19-44; SoC nets on sch p6
+		 * L124-125). DP-REXT = R68 3.01K (sch p6 L144-145). The DP-HPD pad
+		 * (sch p6 L127) appears on no other schematic page, i.e. it is not
+		 * routed to the connector -- in DP alt mode HPD arrives over CC as a
+		 * DisplayPort Attention message, not on a wire.
+		 *
+		 * Clock and reset are the SERDES-wide ones, shared with combophy0 and
+		 * serdes_ccu. The reset is shared on purpose: those two hold it
+		 * deasserted for the running USB3 controller, and this node must never
+		 * be able to take it away. No rate is assigned here -- combophy0 owns
+		 * the 100 MHz serdes-phy-cfg rate.
+		 *
+		 * No consumer yet. When the DP transmitter (eDP0 @0x05740000) lands it
+		 * references this as its second phy, the vendor's shape being
+		 * phys = <&combo0_dp>, <&aux_hpd>; phy-names = "dp-phy", "aux-phy";
+		 * The driver touches no register until that consumer calls phy_init().
+		 */
+		aux_hpd: phy@6c01e00 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-aux-hpd-phy";
+			reg = <0x0 0x06c01e00 0x0 0x200>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_SERDES_PHY_CFG>;
+			clock-names = "serdes";
+			/* shared: combophy0 and serdes_ccu hold it deasserted too */
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_SERDES>;
+			#phy-cells = <0>;
+			status = "okay";
 		};
 
 		/*
